1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,504
Let's see ... What were we going to talk about today ...

2
00:00:06,550 --> 00:00:10,463
I forgot.

3
00:00:10,463 --> 00:00:19,898
Ah! I remembered! we are going to talk about memories.

4
00:00:19,898 --> 00:00:24,359
Recuerdos and memorias are synonyms.

5
00:00:24,359 --> 00:00:29,742
They are two words that have the same meaning.

6
00:00:29,742 --> 00:00:38,811
Memories also refers to a book: my grandfather is writing his memoirs.

7
00:00:38,811 --> 00:00:44,209
So today we are going to talk about memories and memories.

8
00:00:44,209 --> 00:00:50,805
Our memories are the only ones that allow us to return to the past.

9
00:00:50,805 --> 00:00:58,392
And the thing is that going back to the past is impossible, there is no time machine -

10
00:00:58,392 --> 00:01:01,880
Except with our memories.

11
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:05,451
And it's something we don't think about much.

12
00:01:05,451 --> 00:01:09,728
That our memories allow us to return to the past.

13
00:01:09,947 --> 00:01:14,614
So let's go back to the past. Right now.

14
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:19,436
What is your first memory?

15
00:01:19,436 --> 00:01:24,118
What is the earliest memory you have?

16
00:01:24,767 --> 00:01:28,887
Go as far back as you can in time.

17
00:01:28,887 --> 00:01:34,782
What is the first thing you remember? Leave it to me in a comment.

18
00:01:34,782 --> 00:01:41,832
In my case, my earliest memory dates back to when I was ...

19
00:01:41,832 --> 00:01:45,668
Pf ... I don't know. How old could I be.

20
00:01:45,843 --> 00:01:51,537
3? 3 years? Maybe?

21
00:01:51,537 --> 00:01:57,193
2, 3 years? Something like that, I think it was around that age,

22
00:01:57,193 --> 00:02:02,317
because I still used the baby carriage.

23
00:02:02,490 --> 00:02:06,572
I don't know if I could walk, I think I could-

24
00:02:06,572 --> 00:02:13,817
But I used the baby carriage and I didn't walk, I just sat.

25
00:02:13,817 --> 00:02:20,716
So I was coming home sitting in the stroller,

26
00:02:21,310 --> 00:02:26,500
and I think it was my grandmother who was pushing the cart-

27
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:35,063
and I remember that moment- which I do not know how I can remember, because I was just 2, 3 years old...

28
00:02:35,063 --> 00:02:36,845
Really I do not know.

29
00:02:36,845 --> 00:02:42,516
And suddenly I feel something on my foot,

30
00:02:42,516 --> 00:02:48,804
like a tingling or something that was itching me a lot - I was itching,

31
00:02:48,804 --> 00:02:57,136
and I was trying to scratch my foot - because it wouldn't go away, that tingling wouldn't go away.

32
00:02:57,136 --> 00:03:03,637
I thought I had pebbles from playing in the park, inside my shoe.

33
00:03:03,637 --> 00:03:08,026
So I tried to remove my shoe, but there was nothing.

34
00:03:08,026 --> 00:03:14,001
And I kept scratching my foot: oh oh oh oh, it won't go away, this itch won't go away.

35
00:03:14,001 --> 00:03:18,364
And I don't remember much else, that's all I remember.

36
00:03:18,364 --> 00:03:24,493
Many times they tell us that our earliest memories ,

37
00:03:24,493 --> 00:03:28,921
our most distant memories, are not ours.

38
00:03:28,921 --> 00:03:34,028
But we have them because we see them in a photo,

39
00:03:34,028 --> 00:03:37,864
or our mother, our father tells us.

40
00:03:37,864 --> 00:03:42,687
They tell us: when you were little you did this.

41
00:03:42,687 --> 00:03:50,282
And we think we remember it, but actually someone else has told us.

42
00:03:50,282 --> 00:03:55,177
So here I present my next question:

43
00:03:55,330 --> 00:03:58,946
Would you trust your memory?

44
00:03:58,946 --> 00:04:04,337
Memories and recollections are very malleable,

45
00:04:04,337 --> 00:04:09,877
very easy to distort and change.

46
00:04:09,877 --> 00:04:18,000
And it doesn't even take another person to change our memories, to distort them.

47
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,687
They are distorted by something as simple as time.

48
00:04:22,687 --> 00:04:31,339
Time makes our memories mold to our thoughts and perceptions of the world.

49
00:04:31,339 --> 00:04:39,198
So even though I'm convinced of something, I don't know if I would trust myself.

50
00:04:39,198 --> 00:04:41,850
Because I can be wrong.

51
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:48,151
I mean, I don't even remember the color of my murderer in Among Us,

52
00:04:48,151 --> 00:04:54,000
to go around accusing: It was black! It was white!

53
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:01,197
No, I can't trust my memory. One cannot trust.

54
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:10,132
So, if you were given the option to have super memory,

55
00:05:10,132 --> 00:05:12,958
would you have it or not?

56
00:05:12,958 --> 00:05:20,600
And by having super memory I mean you can remember everything.

57
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:26,040
Everything, without making an effort. Everything that has happened in your life,

58
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:30,424
you just remember them. You don't forget it.

59
00:05:30,424 --> 00:05:37,105
For some things it would be great, like for exams.

60
00:05:37,105 --> 00:05:43,654
Imagine reading the book once and that's it.

61
00:05:43,654 --> 00:05:46,085
You already remember everything.

62
00:05:46,085 --> 00:05:50,540
You don't need to study, you don't have to study.

63
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:53,217
I remember everything.

64
00:05:53,217 --> 00:06:00,486
That would be ... Wow. Where do I have to sign? I'm signing up, right?

65
00:06:00,486 --> 00:06:06,680
But, I think it would also have its cons:

66
00:06:06,770 --> 00:06:13,691
Imagine remembering everything that has happened in your life,

67
00:06:13,691 --> 00:06:18,000
including the bad moments.

68
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:27,952
Imagine having to always live with the memory of a relative who has died.

69
00:06:27,952 --> 00:06:30,643
Someone you loved very much.

70
00:06:30,643 --> 00:06:37,334
Or imagine that you have to live for a long time with the memory

71
00:06:37,334 --> 00:06:45,000
of the feelings you had for your ex boyfriend or for your ex girlfriend,

72
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:51,522
for your previous partner, those feelings of love, and never forget them.

73
00:06:52,390 --> 00:06:57,000
It would be horrible, for that part it would be horrible.

74
00:06:57,520 --> 00:06:59,610
This exists.

75
00:06:59,610 --> 00:07:06,779
There are people who have this 'super power', but it is not a super power,

76
00:07:06,779 --> 00:07:11,042
it's a disease called hyperthymesia.

77
00:07:11,340 --> 00:07:17,452
According to the internet, people with hyperthymesia can remember

78
00:07:17,452 --> 00:07:23,868
all the events that they have personally experienced in their life.

79
00:07:23,868 --> 00:07:30,088
A person with hyperthymesia can be given a date

80
00:07:30,088 --> 00:07:35,733
and they will describe all the events that occurred that day:

81
00:07:35,733 --> 00:07:43,918
the weather and many trivial details that many people would not remember.

82
00:07:43,918 --> 00:07:49,354
This is what hyperthymesic people can do.

83
00:07:50,250 --> 00:07:57,207
I don't think there are many ... and obviously I've never met one.

84
00:07:57,207 --> 00:08:02,829
But I wonder what it would be like to have hyperthymesia.

85
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:07,229
Do they consider it a good thing or a bad thing?

86
00:08:07,229 --> 00:08:08,938
What do you think?

87
00:08:08,938 --> 00:08:14,757
Do you think having a super memory is good or bad?

88
00:08:14,757 --> 00:08:23,856
And then we also have the opposite of super memory and the ability to remember everything:

89
00:08:23,856 --> 00:08:29,194
Not remembering anything at all.

90
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:35,318
I think this has no positive side,

91
00:08:35,318 --> 00:08:42,444
unless your life is not good or you have done something very bad,

92
00:08:42,444 --> 00:08:48,032
and you want to forget it, I think that apart from those times ...

93
00:08:48,032 --> 00:08:52,180
Nobody would like to lose their memory,

94
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:59,722
and this is a much more common and well-known disease called Alzheimer's.

95
00:08:59,722 --> 00:09:07,015
Families that have a member with Alzheimer's have a really hard time.

96
00:09:07,015 --> 00:09:16,099
It is very painful for a person very close to you to lose all their memories.

97
00:09:16,099 --> 00:09:23,167
And in fact this is a resource that many book writers use,

98
00:09:23,167 --> 00:09:27,255
to create a dramatic moment:

99
00:09:27,255 --> 00:09:34,833
When the protagonist forgets his friends, or his partner.

100
00:09:34,833 --> 00:09:39,401
It is something that I would not wish on anyone.

101
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:44,303
Luckily, -I knock on wood- Do I have wood?

102
00:09:44,303 --> 00:09:47,459
I don't have wood, I need to touch wood.

103
00:09:47,518 --> 00:09:59,399
Well, I knock on wood, because no loved one or someone close to me can do that.

104
00:09:59,399 --> 00:10:00,269
Please.

105
00:10:00,387 --> 00:10:06,919
For all of this I would say that humans have the perfect balance.

106
00:10:06,919 --> 00:10:10,951
We have memories.

107
00:10:10,951 --> 00:10:16,808
We are able to remember many things that happen in our life.

108
00:10:16,808 --> 00:10:20,923
But we don't remember everything.

109
00:10:20,923 --> 00:10:26,808
We do not remember the answers for the exam ...

110
00:10:26,808 --> 00:10:34,177
But we do not remember certain painful moments in our life either.

111
00:10:34,177 --> 00:10:39,433
Because we have the ability to forget.

112
00:10:39,433 --> 00:10:44,809
But at the same time we do not forget everything.

113
00:10:44,809 --> 00:10:54,229
We remember one part and the other part we forget, and we have a balance ...

114
00:10:54,229 --> 00:10:57,000
which I would say is correct.

115
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:03,817
I don't know if it's the best or the worst, but I think it's a good balance.

116
00:11:03,817 --> 00:11:04,867
It's okay.

117
00:11:05,460 --> 00:11:12,275
And that's it, up till hereTodo y Nada, episode number 2.

118
00:11:12,363 --> 00:11:19,662
Now click here, and subscribe for more Spanish.

119
00:11:19,850 --> 00:11:24,445
And if you don't feel like it then ... You should.

120
00:11:24,445 --> 00:11:28,461
You should still subscribe.